nyra <nyra@gmx.net> wrote in message news:<3F67BACB.A79BD37E@gmx.net>...
> Being able to cast your healing spells is valuable - only use metallic
> body armour if this gives you a _big_ AC bonus: being able to heal
> yourself and your pet is the one big asset you have in the early game.
> Pick up any sort of weapon and spell-friendly armour you can find;
> getting some daggers as throwing weapons is a good idea, as is keeping
> your pet alife (hoping that it takes out the more troublesome monsters
> until you can start to fight things yourself).

This is also my advice; finding a weapon better than the scalpel helps
tremendously.  I usually try to find an elven dagger, or any weapon
that I could get at least basic skill in.  Once you find a suitable
weapon, practice it on the dlvl 1 and 2 monsters until you reach basic
skill.  Another piece of advice is, don't ignore your wand of sleep;
it can save your life in many situations.
 
>From what I have found, once a healer finishes the Quest, the rest of
the game is relatively easy.  The Healer artifact, the Staff of
Aesculapius, is definitely one of the best weapons.  The hard part
lies in getting to the quest and surviving it.

